**Read:** [International Stratigraphic Guide, abridged version — ICS](../sources/International-Stratigraphic-Guide.lingua#r_6NAYJE2KFXGRNCWP6TEY7AZE7J), the complete abridged guide; [Introduction to Historical Geology — Estrada and contributing authors](../sources/Introduction-to-Historical-Geology.lingua#r_1JKSSECEDW0M9QG30TJ1D9KHZD), the complete sequence, correlation, and time-unit material; [Physical Geology, second edition — Steven Earle](../sources/Physical-Geology-Earle.lingua#r_5GWEYVQGR176TXMQT0S881ZSKX), chapters 6 and 8’s stratigraphic relationships.

A stratigraphic unit organizes a particular kind of evidence. Lithostratigraphic units describe bodies defined through lithological properties/relations; biostratigraphic and other units use other criteria; chronostratigraphic units organize the rock record by time relationships. Learn the definitions and how the units relate rather than treating every formation boundary as a global time boundary.

Measured sections preserve thickness, lithology, structures, fossils, contacts, gaps, and observations supporting interpretation. Correlation between sections requires explicit evidence, uncertainty, and possible lateral facies changes or missing intervals. A similar-looking bed can be diachronous, reworked, or unrelated; a widespread marker also needs identification and context.

Distinguish a physical boundary, the observation used to recognize it, and its inferred temporal significance. Use the official stratigraphic guide for terminology, stratotypes, and relationships, and compare with the actual map/report convention. A regional schematic cannot supply every local contact or duration.

**Work:** construct an original small hypothetical pair of measured sections with one erosion surface and a lateral change. Propose correlations supported by different evidence and show where ambiguity remains. Explain how one independent chronological or fossil constraint changes the interpretation without forcing every lithological contact into a synchronous surface.

Read the entire abridged guide and the corresponding historical chapters. The later basin and sequence work deepens geometry/process interpretation, while the paleontology and Earth-history specialties retain their own fuller methods and research.

